If I wanted to become the greatest student of maneuver warfare in human history, I would build a comprehensive vocabulary covering military theory, operational art, strategy, logistics, intelligence, command philosophy, history, and the psychology of warfare.

The goal would not simply be to understand how armies move. It would be to understand how military organizations think, adapt, create advantages, and achieve strategic objectives.

Below is a military studies glossary organized by subject. It emphasizes conceptual and historical understanding rather than instructions for conducting real-world attacks.

# The ultimate maneuver warfare glossary

## 1. Foundational concepts

These are the terms you must understand before studying maneuver warfare at an advanced level.

| Term | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| Maneuver Warfare | A military philosophy emphasizing disruption, adaptability, initiative, and the achievement of objectives through positional and psychological advantages. |
| Attrition Warfare | A military approach that seeks to wear down an opponent's forces and resources over time. |
| Mission Command | A leadership philosophy that gives subordinate commanders freedom to exercise judgment within the commander's overall intent. |
| Initiative | The ability to influence events rather than simply react to an opponent. |
| Tempo | The relative pace at which military organizations make decisions and conduct operations. |
| Operational Art | The connection between strategic objectives and tactical military actions. |
| Tactics | The theory and practice of employing forces in engagements and battles. |
| Strategy | The application of resources and national power toward achieving political objectives. |
| Grand Strategy | The coordination of military, diplomatic, economic, informational, and political instruments of national power. |
| Decisive Point | A condition, event, or factor that creates a significant advantage toward achieving an objective. |
| Center of Gravity | A source of strength or power that provides an actor with freedom of action, physical strength, or will to fight. |
| Culmination | The point at which a military operation can no longer sustain its current level of effectiveness. |
| Friction | The accumulation of difficulties, uncertainty, mistakes, and unforeseen events that complicate military operations. |
| Fog of War | The uncertainty surrounding military situations due to incomplete, inaccurate, or delayed information. |
| Unity of Effort | Coordination among different organizations toward a common objective. |

